Close the selinuxfs hidden-type existence oracle on every write node.

Writing a context to /sys/fs/selinux/{context,access,create,relabel,user,member}
or to /sys/fs/selinux/validatetrans tells the caller whether the TYPE exists in
the loaded policy: EINVAL means security_context_to_sid() could not resolve it,
anything else means it could. On a rooted kernel `u:r:ksu:s0` resolves, so one
write is a root check. These guards force EINVAL for the covered types, and
filter the reply so a computed context never echoes one back either.

MATCH THE PARSED FIELD, NEVER THE RAW BUFFER

The previous revision ran strnstr() over the whole write buffer. Every handler
parses with a fixed-arity sscanf() and ignores whatever follows the last field,
and security_context_to_sid() stops at the first NUL. So both of

    write("u:r:untrusted_app:s0 u:object_r:foo:s0 1 :ksu:")   # trailing junk
    write("u:r:untrusted_app:s0\0:ksu:")                      # junk past a NUL

answered EINVAL on a patched kernel and OK on a stock one, while the same junk
spelled ":kzu:" answered OK on both. Stock cannot tell those apart -- measured,
every trailing-junk variant returns OK uniformly there -- so the cloak itself
became the detector. That is the same inversion common/_hook/README.md records
for the attr guard's old placement, arriving by a different route.

The gate is therefore two functions, not one:

  sel_ctx_hidden(ctx)         takes ONE already-parsed, NUL-terminated context.
                              Every write handler calls it, after its own
                              sscanf(), on the fields the handler actually
                              parsed -- so a well-formed-plus-junk query
                              behaves exactly as it does on stock.

  sel_hidden_bytes(buf, len)  the length-bounded form, used ONLY on the reply
                              selinux_transaction_write() is about to hand
                              back. That buffer is kernel-generated and
                              sel_write_user() embeds NULs in it, so a C-string
                              scan would stop at the first one and miss every
                              context after it.

THE DISPATCHER IS NOT HOOKED ON THE WAY IN

The deleted base variant guarded selinux_transaction_write() -- the generic
dispatcher -- ahead of every handler's own avc_has_perm(). For a domain that
lacks the permission (isolated_app, sdk_sandbox) stock answers EACCES for ANY
string, so a guard there answered EINVAL-for-hidden and EACCES-for-garbage:
the same inversion again, from an unprivileged app. Every guard here sits AFTER
the handler's avc_has_perm() and immediately before its first
security_context_*_to_sid(), which is exactly where stock rejects an absent
type. The reply filter is the one thing that still runs in the dispatcher, and
it runs on the way OUT, after write_op has already returned.

/sys/fs/selinux/access still works for proxies -- see the README's note on
servicemanager and why the uid gate is `>= 2000` and must not be widened.

COVERAGE

sel_write_context, sel_write_validatetrans, sel_write_access, sel_write_create,
sel_write_relabel, sel_write_user, sel_write_member, plus the reply filter in
selinux_transaction_write(). validatetrans has its own file_operations and was
NOT covered by the deleted dispatcher-entry variant; it is covered here.

The type list is mirrored in security/selinux/hooks.c and security/selinux/avc.c
-- adding a type means editing those too. See common/_hook/README.md.

APPLIES TO

5.10, 5.15 and 6.1, whose selinuxfs.c still threads `struct selinux_state *`
through avc_has_perm() and `state` through security_context_to_sid() /
security_context_str_to_sid(). The guards themselves are identical to
hide_selinux_selinuxfs_6_12.patch; only the trailing context lines differ, which
is exactly what keeps the two files mutually exclusive.

This is the refit that replaced the deleted base variant. The base one hooked
the generic dispatcher ahead of every avc_has_perm() and matched the raw write
buffer -- see the two sections above for why both of those were oracles in their
own right -- and it had no reply filter at all, so a computed context could echo
a hidden type straight back out of /sys/fs/selinux/create.

Verified with `patch -p1 -F0 --dry-run` on android12-5.10 (5.10.264),
android13-5.15 (5.15.211) and android14-6.1 (6.1.176), and confirmed to FAIL on
android15-6.6 and android16-6.12. Generated by editing the real file and running
`diff -up`, never by hand.

security/selinux/selinuxfs.o compiles clean on all three (ARCH=arm64 LLVM=1).
--- a/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
+++ b/security/selinux/selinuxfs.c
@@ -674,6 +674,52 @@ static const struct file_operations sel_
 	.llseek		= generic_file_llseek,
 };
 
+/*
+ * Shared hidden-type gate for the selinuxfs write handlers below. The type
+ * list lives here and is mirrored in security/selinux/hooks.c
+ * (selinux_setprocattr / selinux_lsm_setattr / selinux_inode_setxattr) and in
+ * security/selinux/avc.c (slow_avc_audit) -- adding a type means editing all
+ * of them.
+ *
+ * TWO forms, and the difference between them is the whole point:
+ *
+ *   sel_ctx_hidden()   takes ONE already-parsed, NUL-terminated context and is
+ *                      what every write handler below uses. The handlers parse
+ *                      with fixed-arity sscanf() and ignore anything after the
+ *                      last field, and security_context_to_sid() stops at the
+ *                      first NUL. A gate that scanned the raw write buffer
+ *                      instead would reject "<well-formed query> :ksu:" and
+ *                      "<valid ctx>\0:ksu:" where stock accepts both -- so
+ *                      appending junk would answer EINVAL for a hidden type
+ *                      and OK for every other, which is a discrimination stock
+ *                      cannot make and turns the cloak into the detector.
+ *                      Match what the handler parsed, never the buffer it
+ *                      parsed from.
+ *
+ *   sel_hidden_bytes() is the length-bounded form, used ONLY on the reply
+ *                      selinux_transaction_write() is about to hand back. That
+ *                      buffer is kernel-generated, and sel_write_user() embeds
+ *                      NULs in it, so a C-string scan would stop at the first
+ *                      one and miss every context after it.
+ *
+ * Call either only AFTER the handler's own avc_has_perm() has passed, so a
+ * domain that lacks the permission still gets the stock EACCES and the cloak
+ * never becomes the tell.
+ */
+static bool sel_hidden_bytes(const char *buf, size_t size)
+{
+	if (__kuid_val(current_uid()) < 2000)
+		return false;
+	return strnstr(buf, ":ksu:", size) || strnstr(buf, ":ksu_file:", size) ||
+	       strnstr(buf, ":lsposed_file:", size) ||
+	       strnstr(buf, ":zygisk_file:", size);
+}
+
+static bool sel_ctx_hidden(const char *ctx)
+{
+	return ctx && sel_hidden_bytes(ctx, strlen(ctx));
+}
